+Stella -framesnap: completed emulated-frame capture
+===================================================
+
+Usage:
+  ./stella -framesnap '1,5-10,12' game.bin
+
+The PNGs are saved in Stella's normal snapshot directory (-snapsavedir).
+Each name includes the ROM name, an actual per-run wall-clock timestamp
+and microsecond epoch component, plus the emulated frame index in 8-digit hex.
+Example:
+  game_framesnap_2026-10-10_12-34-56_1791670496123456_0000000C.png
+
+The images are 320 x TIA image height (160 native TIA color clocks
+horizontally doubled for display) and lossless indexed PNGs. The active
+Stella TIA palette supplies the 256 RGB palette entries, but no phosphor,
+NTSC filter, scanlines, bezel, or UI effects are applied.
+
+Implementation:
+ - TIA::onFrameComplete() tests -framesnap and synchronously saves the
+   completed myFrontBuffer, before the next frame can overwrite it.
+ - PNGLibrary::saveEmulatedFrame() encodes that buffer directly as a
+   paletted PNG. It NEVER reads the last displayed framebuffer.
+ - The earlier TIASurface::render() capture hook is removed. F12 and
+   the existing snapshot path remain untouched.
+ - PNG failures are logged to stderr with the affected frame index.
+
+NOTE: Disk I/O for selected frames runs on the emulation thread. A long
+range will temporarily slow real-time playback, but will not skip frames
+because of the display render loop. Each selected frame is saved before
+emulation proceeds to the next one.
+
+Build check in this environment:
+ - src/emucore/tia/TIA.cxx, src/common/PNGLibrary.cxx and
+   src/emucore/TIASurface.cxx compiled successfully.
+ - Full compilation / emulator execution was not possible here because
+   the SDL3 development headers and runtime are absent.
+ - Rebuild on your Stella machine with make -j$(nproc).
